We shall find that most of the facts about the topology of the spaces we are concerned with here can be expressed in terms of least upper bounds (lubs). It is not always the case, however, that lubs are limits. Thus, for a partially ordered set $X$, we impose a further restriction on its topology beyond condition (i) for saying when a subset $U$ is open:

(ii) whenever $S \subseteq X$ is *directed*, $\bigsqcup S$ exists, and $\bigsqcup S \in U$, then $S \cap U \neq \emptyset$.

By a directed subset of $X$ we of course mean that it is directed in the sense of the partial ordering $\sqsubseteq$. Note that in this paper directed sets are always *non-empty*.

Scott-open sets are exactly the open sets of `scottTopologicalSpace`.

On a complete lattice $D$ we use the lattice order $\le$ (for the specialization order on $D$ with the Scott topology, this agrees with $\sqsubseteq$; see `SpecializationPreorder.scott_iff`). Condition (i) is that $U$ be an *upper set* (an upset): if $a \in U$ and $a \le b$, then $b \in U$. The formalization packages (i) and (ii) for subsets of $D$ as `ScottOpen`. The lemmas below are the library tools that justify that equivalence.

**Definition (`ScottOpen`).** A subset $U \subseteq D$ is **Scott-open** when $U$ is an upper set and, for every nonempty directed $S \subseteq D$, if $\sup S \in U$ then $S \cap U \neq \emptyset$.

**Lemma (`ScottOpen.isUpperSet`).** If $U$ is Scott-open, then $U$ is an upper set.

**Proof.** This is the first component of the definition. $\square$

**Lemma (`ScottOpen.inter`).** If $U$ and $V$ are Scott-open, then $U \cap V$ is Scott-open.

**Proof.** $U \cap V$ is an upper set. If $\sup S \in U \cap V$ for directed nonempty $S$, then $\sup S \in U$ and $\sup S \in V$. By Scott-openness of $U$ and $V$ there are $s_1, s_2 \in S$ with $s_1 \in U$ and $s_2 \in V$. Directedness gives $s_3 \in S$ with $s_3 \ge s_1, s_2$, hence $s_3 \in U \cap V$. $\square$

**Lemma (`ScottOpen.univ`).** The whole lattice $D$ is Scott-open.

**Proof.** $D$ is an upper set. If $\sup S \in D$ for nonempty $S$, any $s \in S$ lies in $D$. $\square$

**Lemma (`ScottOpen.sUnion`).** If every member of a family $\mathcal{C}$ of subsets of $D$ is Scott-open, then $\bigcup \mathcal{C}$ is Scott-open.

**Proof.** A union of upper sets is an upper set. If $\sup S$ lies in some $U \in \mathcal{C}$ with $U$ Scott-open, pick $s \in S \cap U$; then $s \in \bigcup \mathcal{C}$. $\square$

**Lemma (`ScottOpen.iff_dirSupInacc`).** For $U \subseteq D$,

$$
\text{$U$ is Scott-open} \iff \text{$U$ is an upper set and $U$ is inaccessible under directed sups}
$$

(in Mathlib: `IsUpperSet U ∧ DirSupInacc U`).

**Proof.** Both sides unfold to the same upset condition and the same “if $\sup S \in U$ for directed nonempty $S$, then $S \cap U \neq \emptyset$” clause, phrased either with `sSup` or with `IsLUB`. $\square$

**Lemma (`ScottOpen.isOpen_iff`).** With the Scott topology `scottTopologicalSpace` on $D$, a set $U \subseteq D$ is topologically open iff $U$ is Scott-open.

**Proof.** Rewrite via `ScottOpen.iff_dirSupInacc` and the definition of `scottTopologicalSpace` as `Topology.scott D Set.univ`; conclude with Mathlib’s `IsScott.isOpen_iff_isUpperSet_and_dirSupInaccOn`. $\square$

**Lemma (`ScottOpen.not_le`).** For any $L \in D$, the set $U = \{ z \in D : z \not\le L \}$ is Scott-open.

**Proof.** If $a \in U$ and $a \le b$, then $b \not\le L$ (otherwise $a \le b \le L$). If $\sup S \in U$ for directed nonempty $S$ but $S \cap U = \emptyset$, then every $s \in S$ satisfies $s \le L$, so $\sup S \le L$, contradicting $\sup S \in U$. $\square$

**Lemma (`ScottOpen.wayBelow`).** For $x \in D$, the set

$$
\{ z \in D : \exists U \subseteq D,\ \text{$U$ Scott-open} \land z \in U \land U \subseteq \{ w : x \le w \} \}
$$

is Scott-open. Once the way-below relation $x \ll y$ is defined (`WayBelow`), this set is $\{ z : x \ll z \}$; see `WayBelow.lean`.

**Proof.** If $a$ belongs to a Scott-open $U$ with $U \subseteq \{ w : x \le w \}$ and $a \le b$, then $b \in U$ by the upset property, so $b$ is in the same set. If $\sup S$ belongs to the set, pick a witness Scott-open $U$ with $\sup S \in U \subseteq \{ w : x \le w \}$. By Scott-openness of $U$, some $s \in S$ lies in $U$, and the same $U$ witnesses $s$ in $\{ z : x \ll z \}$ in the existential form. $\square$
